window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["bedrock"]=[{"id":"bedrock-f0001","card":"q-bedrock-001-1-what-bedrock-is","sec":"bedrock-s002","q":"What is Amazon Bedrock and what problem does it solve?","a":"<p>Bedrock is a fully managed AWS service that gives you foundation models from several providers (Anthropic Claude, Amazon Titan and Nova, Mistral, Meta Llama and others) behind one API. You do not host GPUs or model servers. It removes the work of hosting, scaling and integrating each model vendor separately.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0002","card":"q-bedrock-001-1-what-bedrock-is","sec":"bedrock-s002","q":"Why would a team choose Bedrock over calling a single model vendor's API directly?","a":"<p>One AWS API, IAM authentication, billing and logging cover many models, so you can switch or mix models without rewriting integration code. Data stays inside your AWS account and its controls (VPC endpoints, KMS, CloudTrail). The trade-off is that new vendor features can reach Bedrock later than the vendor's own API, and some models are not offered.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0003","card":"q-bedrock-001-1-what-bedrock-is","sec":"bedrock-s002","q":"What is the benefit of the unified API when you want to upgrade or swap a model?","a":"<p>You change the model ID and keep the surrounding code, so moving from an expensive model to a cheaper one, or adopting a newer model, takes little change. The Converse API makes this easiest because its request and response shape is the same across supported models. With the raw <code>InvokeModel</code> API the request body is still model-specific.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0004","card":"q-bedrock-001-1-what-bedrock-is","sec":"bedrock-s002","q":"Name the main ways to customize model behavior in Bedrock and when you would use each.","a":"<p>Prompt engineering and system prompts first, because they are cheapest. RAG through Knowledge Bases when the model needs your private or changing data. Fine-tuning or continued pre-training (available for selected models, as of 2025) when you need a consistent style or domain behavior that prompts cannot give. Fine-tune only after prompting and RAG fall short, because it costs more and the data goes stale.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0005","card":"q-bedrock-001-1-what-bedrock-is","sec":"bedrock-s002","q":"Why do many teams experiment in the Bedrock console before writing code?","a":"<p>The console lets you try models, system prompts, parameters, knowledge bases and agents with no code, so you find a working setup quickly. You then move it to the API or an SDK. The risk is leaving it there, since console settings are not versioned or repeatable like code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0006","card":"q-bedrock-001-1-what-bedrock-is","sec":"bedrock-s002","q":"How does Bedrock on-demand pricing work, and why does model choice matter for cost?","a":"<p>On-demand inference is billed by input and output tokens, and prices differ a lot between models. Output tokens usually cost more than input tokens, and large models cost several times more than small ones, so routing simple tasks to a small model cuts cost the most. Provisioned throughput is the alternative for steady, high volume.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0007","card":"q-bedrock-002-2-core-features-and-concepts","sec":"bedrock-s003","q":"What is a token, and why does it matter when you use a foundation model?","a":"<p>A token is the basic unit of text a model reads and writes, often a word piece rather than a whole word. Pricing, context window size and maximum response length are all counted in tokens, so long prompts raise cost and latency and can exceed the limit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0008","card":"q-bedrock-002-2-core-features-and-concepts","sec":"bedrock-s003","q":"What is the difference between model parameters and inference parameters?","a":"<p>Model parameters are the learned weights inside the model, set by training or fine-tuning. Inference parameters (temperature, top P, top K, max tokens, stop sequences) are settings you pass on each request to control how output is sampled. You change inference parameters per call. Changing model parameters needs fine-tuning.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0009","card":"q-bedrock-002-2-core-features-and-concepts","sec":"bedrock-s003","q":"What does temperature do, and what value would you choose for data extraction versus marketing copy?","a":"<p>Temperature scales how random token selection is. Low values (near 0) make output more deterministic, which suits extraction, classification and code. Higher values (0.7 to 1) give more varied output, which suits brainstorming and copy. Even at 0, identical output is not guaranteed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0010","card":"q-bedrock-002-2-core-features-and-concepts","sec":"bedrock-s003","q":"How do top P and top K differ?","a":"<p>Both limit which tokens the model may pick. Top K keeps only the K most likely tokens. Top P (nucleus sampling) keeps the smallest set of tokens whose probabilities add up to P. Top K is a fixed count and top P adapts to how confident the model is. Not every model supports top K. It is usually advised to tune temperature or top P, not both at once.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0011","card":"q-bedrock-002-2-core-features-and-concepts","sec":"bedrock-s003","q":"What does the max tokens setting do, and what happens if you set it too low?","a":"<p>It caps how many tokens the model may produce. If it is too low, the answer is cut off mid-sentence and the stop reason reports that the length limit was hit. A high cap costs nothing by itself, because you pay only for tokens actually produced. Set it to what the task needs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0012","card":"q-bedrock-002-2-core-features-and-concepts","sec":"bedrock-s003","q":"What are embeddings and why do they matter for RAG?","a":"<p>An embedding is a vector of numbers that represents the meaning of text or an image, so similar content has nearby vectors. RAG embeds your documents and the user question, then finds the closest document vectors in a vector store. Bedrock offers embedding models such as Amazon Titan Text Embeddings and Cohere Embed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0013","card":"q-bedrock-002-2-core-features-and-concepts","sec":"bedrock-s003","q":"What problem does RAG solve, and what are its drawbacks?","a":"<p>RAG reduces hallucination and stale answers by retrieving relevant passages from your own data and giving them to the model as context. It also avoids retraining when data changes. Drawbacks: quality depends on chunking and retrieval, retrieval adds latency and a larger prompt costs more, and the index must be kept in sync.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0014","card":"q-bedrock-002-2-core-features-and-concepts","sec":"bedrock-s003","q":"When would you choose RAG over fine-tuning?","a":"<p>Choose RAG when the knowledge changes often, must be cited, or is access-controlled per user. Choose fine-tuning to change the model's style, format or specialist behavior, not to add facts. Fine-tuning bakes data into the weights, so updates need retraining and answers cannot show their source.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0015","card":"q-bedrock-002-2-core-features-and-concepts","sec":"bedrock-s003","q":"What is provisioned throughput in Bedrock, and when do you need it?","a":"<p>Provisioned throughput reserves dedicated model capacity (model units) for a fixed hourly price, with optional commitment terms, so throughput and latency are steady. It is required to run inference on a custom (fine-tuned) model, and it suits steady high-volume traffic. For spiky or low traffic, on-demand per-token billing is cheaper.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0016","card":"q-bedrock-002-2-core-features-and-concepts","sec":"bedrock-s003","q":"In Bedrock, what two things must be in place before an account can invoke a model, and what error appears if one is missing?","a":"<p>The model must be available to the account (historically enabled through model access in the console, and AWS has been simplifying this, so check the current flow), and the caller's IAM policy must allow <code>bedrock:InvokeModel</code> on that model. If either is missing, the call fails with an access denied error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0017","card":"q-bedrock-002-2-core-features-and-concepts","sec":"bedrock-s003","q":"What is prompt injection, and how do you reduce the risk in a Bedrock application?","a":"<p>Prompt injection is untrusted text, in user input or retrieved documents, that tries to override your instructions (for example \"ignore your earlier rules\"). Reduce the risk by keeping instructions in the system prompt, treating retrieved content as data, applying Bedrock Guardrails to inputs and outputs, giving agents least-privilege permissions, and validating model output before acting on it. No single control removes the risk.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0018","card":"q-bedrock-003-3-console-and-playground","sec":"bedrock-s004","q":"What is a system prompt and what should go in it?","a":"<p>A system prompt is a set of instructions that applies to the whole conversation, separate from the user's messages. Put the role, rules, output format and style there, such as \"return only JSON\". Because it persists on every turn, it gives consistent behavior without repeating it in each message.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0019","card":"q-bedrock-003-3-console-and-playground","sec":"bedrock-s004","q":"How does a system prompt differ from a user prompt in practice?","a":"<p>The system prompt sets standing behavior and constraints, and the user prompt is the specific task for that turn. Models treat system text as higher-level guidance, but it is not a security boundary: prompt injection from user or retrieved text can still override it. Use Guardrails and input validation for enforcement.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0020","card":"q-bedrock-003-3-console-and-playground","sec":"bedrock-s004","q":"You want a model to return code only, with no explanation, and keep it short. Which settings and prompt text would you use?","a":"<p>Put the rule in the system prompt (\"Only give code. No explanation.\") and set a low max tokens value so the response stays small. The instruction controls what the model writes, and the token cap is a hard backstop that cuts output if the model ignores the instruction, so use both.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0021","card":"q-bedrock-003-3-console-and-playground","sec":"bedrock-s004","q":"In the Bedrock console, how do the Playground and the builder features such as Agents, Flows and Knowledge Bases differ in purpose?","a":"<p>The Playground is for trying models interactively (chat, text, image) with prompts and parameters. The builder features are what you assemble into applications: Agents, Flows, Knowledge Bases and prompt management. Use the Playground to find a prompt, then the builder features to build around it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0022","card":"q-bedrock-003-3-console-and-playground","sec":"bedrock-s004","q":"What is Amazon Bedrock Guardrails and what can it do?","a":"<p>Guardrails checks inputs and model outputs against policies you define: content filters (hate, violence and others), denied topics, word filters, sensitive information and PII filters (block or mask), and contextual grounding checks for RAG. It also works with models outside Bedrock through the <code>ApplyGuardrail</code> API.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0023","card":"q-bedrock-003-3-console-and-playground","sec":"bedrock-s004","q":"What is Amazon Bedrock Flows, and when would you use it instead of an agent?","a":"<p>Flows is a visual builder that chains prompts, models, knowledge bases, Lambda functions and conditions into a fixed workflow you design. Use it when the steps and their order are known and you want predictable, testable execution. Use an agent when the model should decide at run time which steps or tools to use, at the cost of less predictability.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0024","card":"q-bedrock-004-4-compare-mode-and-the-dracula-pattern","sec":"bedrock-s005","q":"What does Compare Mode in the Bedrock Playground let you do, and what should you compare?","a":"<p>It sends the same prompt to two models side by side. Compare output quality, instruction following, latency and cost per call. It is useful for finding the cheapest model that still meets your quality bar, and as a quick manual check before you automate evaluation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0025","card":"q-bedrock-004-4-compare-mode-and-the-dracula-pattern","sec":"bedrock-s005","q":"Why is a working example with expected output more reliable in a prompt than a description in words?","a":"<p>A concrete example shows the exact format, style and behavior, so the model copies the pattern instead of interpreting vague words. This is few-shot prompting. In order of reliability: words only, then words plus format rules, then format rules plus a worked example. The cost is extra input tokens on every call.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0026","card":"q-bedrock-004-4-compare-mode-and-the-dracula-pattern","sec":"bedrock-s005","q":"Why can a larger and a smaller model give different results from the same prompt, and what are the trade-offs?","a":"<p>Larger models follow complex instructions more consistently and cope with gaps in the prompt. Smaller models are faster and cheaper but need clearer, more explicit prompts and may drop elements or formatting. The trade-off is quality and consistency versus cost and latency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0027","card":"q-bedrock-004-4-compare-mode-and-the-dracula-pattern","sec":"bedrock-s005","q":"Why should you treat prompts like software?","a":"<p>Prompts change behavior as code does, so they need version control, review, tests and output validation (JSON schema, XML, compiling the code). Without this, a small wording or model change causes silent regressions that nobody can reproduce. Output validation also makes adopting a cheaper model safe.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0028","card":"q-bedrock-005-the-dracula-pattern-cloud-to-cloud","sec":"bedrock-s006","q":"What is the idea of moving a prompt from a premium model to a cheaper one, and why do it?","a":"<p>You develop a reliable prompt on a strong, expensive model, then refine it until a cheaper model produces equivalent output, and run production on the cheaper model. You pay for the premium model only during development, so per-call cost drops in production. It works best for repeated, well-defined tasks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0029","card":"q-bedrock-005-the-dracula-pattern-cloud-to-cloud","sec":"bedrock-s006","q":"What are the steps for moving a task from a premium model to a cheaper model without losing quality?","a":"<ol>\n  <li>Build and document a robust prompt on the premium model, with output validation. 2) Run the same prompt and test inputs on the cheaper model and note the gaps. 3) Ask the premium model to rewrite the prompt so the cheaper model gives the same output, then repeat and re-validate. 4) Deploy on the cheaper model and keep the tests to catch regressions.</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0030","card":"q-bedrock-005-the-dracula-pattern-cloud-to-cloud","sec":"bedrock-s006","q":"Why must you validate output automatically when moving to a cheaper model?","a":"<p>Reading a few outputs by eye misses edge cases. Automatic checks (JSON or XML schema validation, linting, compiling code, unit tests, a fixed set of test prompts) tell you objectively whether the cheaper model matches the baseline, and they keep catching regressions after later prompt or model changes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0031","card":"q-bedrock-005-the-dracula-pattern-cloud-to-cloud","sec":"bedrock-s006","q":"What can go wrong when moving a prompt to a cheaper model, and how do you avoid it?","a":"<p>Typical failures: no baseline from the premium model to compare against, undocumented prompt edits nobody can reproduce, treating the prompt as loose natural language so output varies, and no quality monitoring after the switch. Avoid them with a baseline test set, versioned prompts, structured output and production monitoring.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0032","card":"q-bedrock-005-the-dracula-pattern-cloud-to-cloud","sec":"bedrock-s006","q":"How can Bedrock choose a model per request instead of you switching by hand?","a":"<p>Bedrock Intelligent Prompt Routing (as of 2025) sends each request to a larger or smaller model in the same family based on predicted complexity, to balance cost and quality. You can also route in your own code by task type. In both cases test routing quality, because a wrong route to a small model gives a worse answer. Monitor quality, latency, cost per request and the rate of wrong routes, because a hard task sent to a weak model degrades answers without any error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0033","card":"q-bedrock-006-the-dracula-pattern-cloud-to-local","sec":"bedrock-s007","q":"Why would you move a prompt from a Bedrock model to a locally hosted open model, for example one run with Ollama?","a":"<p>Reasons: no per-token cost at high volume, data never leaves your machine or network (privacy, legal or health rules), offline use, and batch jobs. Cost moves from variable API fees to fixed hardware and power, and you take over updates, scaling and monitoring.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0034","card":"q-bedrock-006-the-dracula-pattern-cloud-to-local","sec":"bedrock-s007","q":"What are the steps for moving a prompt from a Bedrock model to a local model?","a":"<p>First validate the prompt on a Bedrock model to get a baseline. Run the same prompt on the local model, compare with the baseline, refine the prompt while watching for quality loss, and repeat until the local output matches. Skipping the baseline leaves nothing to compare against.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0035","card":"q-bedrock-006-the-dracula-pattern-cloud-to-local","sec":"bedrock-s007","q":"What hardware considerations apply when you run a model locally instead of on Bedrock?","a":"<p>Model size decides memory needs. Small quantized models can run on CPU for batch or low-urgency work. For low-latency interactive use you generally need a GPU with enough VRAM, such as an NVIDIA card. Underestimating hardware gives slow inference and a poor user experience.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0036","card":"q-bedrock-006-the-dracula-pattern-cloud-to-local","sec":"bedrock-s007","q":"What are the trade-offs between cloud and local models for updates and operations?","a":"<p>Bedrock updates models for you and needs internet access, but your data leaves your network. A local model works offline and keeps data local, but you update, patch, secure and monitor it yourself, and you are limited to the open models you can run.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0037","card":"q-bedrock-007-5-deployment-choices-and-genai-misconcep","sec":"bedrock-s008","q":"How do you decide between a premium cloud model, a cheaper cloud model and a local model for a workload?","a":"<p>Use a premium model for complex or high-stakes tasks where quality matters most, a cheaper model for simple or high-volume tasks, and a local model for privacy-sensitive, offline or batch work. Judge by quality, cost, compliance and control. One application may use several, with a layer that routes between them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0038","card":"q-bedrock-007-5-deployment-choices-and-genai-misconcep","sec":"bedrock-s008","q":"Why is a hybrid of cloud and local models often better than choosing one?","a":"<p>Each option fails some requirement: cloud may be disallowed for regulated data, and local models may lack quality or capacity. A hybrid keeps sensitive or high-volume work local and sends hard or bursty work to Bedrock. The cost is a more complex system with routing logic and two things to operate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0039","card":"q-bedrock-007-5-deployment-choices-and-genai-misconcep","sec":"bedrock-s008","q":"How would you answer the claim that generative AI will fully automate coding?","a":"<p>More machine-written code means more code to review, test and maintain, and more bugs and complexity. The work shifts from typing code to specifying, reviewing, testing and operating it, so debugging, CI/CD, logging, monitoring and code review matter more, not less.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0040","card":"q-bedrock-007-5-deployment-choices-and-genai-misconcep","sec":"bedrock-s008","q":"How would you respond to someone who says large language models are close to general intelligence?","a":"<p>An LLM predicts the next token from patterns in its training data. It is very useful, but it can state wrong answers confidently and has no guaranteed reasoning, so it needs grounding and validation. Treat it as a powerful pattern-based component inside a system with checks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0041","card":"q-bedrock-007-5-deployment-choices-and-genai-misconcep","sec":"bedrock-s008","q":"What is a sensible way for a team to adopt generative AI step by step?","a":"<p>Start with low-risk, easy-to-verify uses: unit test drafts, logging and instrumentation, code review help, summarization. Keep a human in the loop, require tests to pass, and use typed languages and linters to catch mistakes. Widen the scope as you measure results.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0042","card":"q-bedrock-008-6-bedrock-api-bash-to-rust","sec":"bedrock-s009","q":"Why prototype a Bedrock call in Bash with the AWS CLI before writing production code?","a":"<p>The CLI already handles credentials, request signing (SigV4) and transport, so a few lines test the model, request body and response. Once the round trip works, port it to an SDK language. The trade-off is that shell scripts are weak at error handling, testing and distribution.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0043","card":"q-bedrock-008-6-bedrock-api-bash-to-rust","sec":"bedrock-s009","q":"What are the strengths of Rust for a production Bedrock client compared with a Bash script?","a":"<p>Rust builds a single self-contained binary with no runtime to install, has strong typing and explicit error handling with <code>Result</code>, and uses little CPU and memory. Costs: slower to write, longer setup (Cargo and dependencies) and a steeper learning curve. Python or Node SDKs are lighter alternatives when binary size and startup matter less.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0044","card":"q-bedrock-008-6-bedrock-api-bash-to-rust","sec":"bedrock-s009","q":"What is the difference between the <code>bedrock</code> and <code>bedrock-runtime</code> services in the AWS CLI and SDKs?","a":"<p><code>bedrock</code> is the control plane: listing foundation models, managing custom models, guardrails and provisioned throughput. <code>bedrock-runtime</code> is the data plane for inference: <code>InvokeModel</code>, <code>InvokeModelWithResponseStream</code> and <code>Converse</code>. <code>bedrock-agent</code> and <code>bedrock-agent-runtime</code> are the matching pair for agents and knowledge bases.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0045","card":"q-bedrock-008-6-bedrock-api-bash-to-rust","sec":"bedrock-s009","q":"What is the difference between <code>InvokeModel</code> and the Converse API in <code>bedrock-runtime</code>?","a":"<p><code>InvokeModel</code> sends a raw body in the format the specific model expects, so changing models usually means changing the body. <code>Converse</code> uses one message-based request and response format for supported models, with built-in support for system prompts, tool use and multi-turn history. Use <code>Converse</code> for portable code and <code>InvokeModel</code> for model-specific features it does not expose.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0046","card":"q-bedrock-008-6-bedrock-api-bash-to-rust","sec":"bedrock-s009","q":"Why does a long Bedrock answer take a while to appear, and which API option helps the user experience?","a":"<p>The model writes tokens one at a time, so a long answer takes time to finish. <code>InvokeModelWithResponseStream</code> (or <code>ConverseStream</code>) returns tokens as they arrive, so the user sees text immediately. Total time is the same, but perceived latency drops.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0047","card":"q-bedrock-008-6-bedrock-api-bash-to-rust","sec":"bedrock-s009","q":"A Bedrock call from your CLI or SDK fails with an access denied or validation error. What do you check?","a":"<p>Check that the credentials resolve to the right identity and account, that the IAM policy allows <code>bedrock:InvokeModel</code> on that model ARN, that the model is available in that Region, and that the model ID is correct (some models need an inference profile ID instead of the base ID). Then check the request body format for that model.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0048","card":"q-bedrock-008-6-bedrock-api-bash-to-rust","sec":"bedrock-s009","q":"What is a Bedrock inference profile, and why would you use a cross-Region one instead of a plain model ID?","a":"<p>An inference profile is an identifier you pass as the model ID that can route a request to several AWS Regions (a system-defined cross-Region profile) or tag usage for cost tracking (an application profile). Cross-Region profiles raise throughput and resilience under load. The trade-offs are that data may be processed in another Region, which matters for residency rules, and some newer models can only be called through a profile (as of 2025).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0049","card":"q-bedrock-008-6-bedrock-api-bash-to-rust","sec":"bedrock-s009","q":"What does this boto3 call to the Bedrock Converse API do, and where do the system prompt and sampling settings go?","a":"<p>It sends one chat turn to the model named by <code>model_id</code> and prints the reply text. The system prompt goes in <code>system</code>, the conversation in <code>messages</code> (each with a role and content blocks), and sampling settings in <code>inferenceConfig</code>. Read <code>response[\"stopReason\"]</code> too: <code>max_tokens</code> means the answer was cut off, so raise <code>maxTokens</code> or shorten the prompt.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import boto3\n\nclient = boto3.client(&quot;bedrock-runtime&quot;)\nresponse = client.converse(\n    modelId=model_id,\n    system=[{&quot;text&quot;: &quot;Answer in one sentence.&quot;}],\n    messages=[{&quot;role&quot;: &quot;user&quot;, &quot;content&quot;: [{&quot;text&quot;: &quot;What is RAG?&quot;}]}],\n    inferenceConfig={&quot;maxTokens&quot;: 200, &quot;temperature&quot;: 0.0},\n)\nprint(response[&quot;output&quot;][&quot;message&quot;][&quot;content&quot;][0][&quot;text&quot;])</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"bedrock-f0050","card":"q-bedrock-009-bash-hello-world-claude-sh","sec":"bedrock-s010","q":"In a Bash script that calls the AWS CLI, what does this check do?","a":"<p><code>command -v aws</code> prints the path of <code>aws</code> if it is on the <code>PATH</code> and fails if not. <code>&amp;&gt; /dev/null</code> hides its output so only the exit status is used. If the command is missing, the script prints a message and exits with status 1, so callers and CI can detect the failure.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>if ! command -v aws &amp;&gt; /dev/null; then\n    echo &quot;AWS CLI is not installed&quot;\n    exit 1\nfi</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"bedrock-f0051","card":"q-bedrock-009-bash-hello-world-claude-sh","sec":"bedrock-s010","q":"In a Bash script, what does this line do when the script is run with and without an argument?","a":"<p><code>${1:-default}</code> expands to the first argument if it is set and non-empty, otherwise to the text after <code>:-</code>. So <code>./claude.sh \"Tell a joke\"</code> uses the joke prompt, and <code>./claude.sh</code> alone uses the default prompt.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>PROMPT=&quot;${1:-Say hello in a creative way}&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"bedrock-f0052","card":"q-bedrock-009-bash-hello-world-claude-sh","sec":"bedrock-s010","q":"Why does a Bash script that calls <code>aws bedrock-runtime invoke-model</code> create temporary files with <code>mktemp</code>?","a":"<p>The CLI reads the request body from a file (<code>--body file://...</code>) and writes the model response to an output file named as the last argument, so both need paths. <code>mktemp</code> makes unique, safe file names so parallel runs do not collide. Remove them afterwards, ideally with a <code>trap</code>, so they are cleaned up even when the script fails.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>REQUEST_FILE=$(mktemp)\nRESPONSE_FILE=$(mktemp)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"bedrock-f0053","card":"q-bedrock-009-bash-hello-world-claude-sh","sec":"bedrock-s010","q":"In the AWS CLI command <code>aws bedrock-runtime invoke-model</code>, what do <code>--model-id</code>, <code>--content-type</code>, <code>--accept</code> and <code>--body</code> each do?","a":"<p><code>--model-id</code> picks the model to run. <code>--content-type</code> says the request body is JSON and <code>--accept</code> asks for a JSON response. <code>--body</code> is the request payload, often <code>file://path</code> to read it from a file. The body schema depends on the model. In AWS CLI v2, an inline JSON body also needs <code>--cli-binary-format raw-in-base64-out</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0054","card":"q-bedrock-009-bash-hello-world-claude-sh","sec":"bedrock-s010","q":"What request body did the legacy Anthropic Claude v2 text-completion API on Bedrock expect, and how do newer Claude models differ?","a":"<p>Claude v2 used text completions: a <code>prompt</code> wrapped as <code>\\n\\nHuman: ... \\n\\nAssistant:</code>, plus <code>max_tokens_to_sample</code>, <code>temperature</code> and <code>stop_sequences</code>, with the answer in a <code>completion</code> field. Claude 3 and later use the Messages format: <code>anthropic_version</code> set to <code>bedrock-2023-05-31</code>, a <code>messages</code> list, <code>max_tokens</code>, and the answer in <code>content</code>. Claude v2 is legacy (as of 2025), so new code should use Messages or Converse.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0055","card":"q-bedrock-009-bash-hello-world-claude-sh","sec":"bedrock-s010","q":"In a Claude v2 text-completion request body, what is the role of <code>stop_sequences</code>?","a":"<p><code>stop_sequences</code> lists strings that make the model stop as soon as it produces one. Using <code>\"\\n\\nHuman:\"</code> stops the model from inventing the next user turn after its own answer. It also lets you add custom stop strings.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{\n    &quot;prompt&quot;: &quot;\\n\\nHuman: Say hello\\n\\nAssistant:&quot;,\n    &quot;max_tokens_to_sample&quot;: 300,\n    &quot;temperature&quot;: 0.7,\n    &quot;stop_sequences&quot;: [&quot;\\n\\nHuman:&quot;]\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"bedrock-f0056","card":"q-bedrock-009-bash-hello-world-claude-sh","sec":"bedrock-s010","q":"In Bash, how can you pull the answer text out of the JSON file that Bedrock wrote, and what does this one-liner do?","a":"<p>It reads the JSON from standard input with Python's <code>json.load</code> and prints one field, here <code>completion</code> (the Claude v2 field). <code>jq -r .completion \"$RESPONSE_FILE\"</code> does the same without starting Python. The field name depends on the model's response format; for the Messages format it is <code>content[0].text</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>cat &quot;$RESPONSE_FILE&quot; | python3 -c &quot;import sys,json; print(json.load(sys.stdin)[&#x27;completion&#x27;])&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"bedrock-f0057","card":"q-bedrock-009-bash-hello-world-claude-sh","sec":"bedrock-s010","q":"Why does a script start with <code>#!/bin/bash</code> and need <code>chmod +x</code>?","a":"<p>The shebang tells the OS which interpreter runs the file, and <code>chmod +x</code> sets the execute permission, so you can run <code>./claude.sh</code> directly. Without the execute bit you must run <code>bash claude.sh</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0058","card":"q-bedrock-010-rust-hello-world","sec":"bedrock-s011","q":"In a Rust Bedrock client, what is the role of each of these crates: <code>aws-config</code>, <code>aws-sdk-bedrockruntime</code>, <code>tokio</code>, <code>serde_json</code> and <code>anyhow</code>?","a":"<p><code>aws-config</code> loads credentials and Region from the standard AWS chain (environment, profile, role). <code>aws-sdk-bedrockruntime</code> is the typed client for model inference. <code>tokio</code> is the async runtime, required because the SDK is async. <code>serde_json</code> builds and parses JSON bodies. <code>anyhow</code> gives one flexible error type so <code>?</code> works across different error types.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0059","card":"q-bedrock-010-rust-hello-world","sec":"bedrock-s011","q":"In Rust, why does an AWS SDK program need <code>tokio</code>, and what does <code>#[tokio::main]</code> do?","a":"<p>The SDK calls are <code>async</code> and need an executor, and Rust has no built-in runtime. <code>#[tokio::main]</code> is a macro that turns <code>async fn main</code> into a normal <code>main</code> that starts a Tokio runtime and blocks on the body. Without it, <code>async fn main</code> does not compile.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Rust</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>#[tokio::main]\nasync fn main() -&gt; anyhow::Result&lt;()&gt; {\n    // ...\n    Ok(())\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"bedrock-f0060","card":"q-bedrock-010-rust-hello-world","sec":"bedrock-s011","q":"In the AWS SDK for Rust, how do you create a client with default credentials and Region?","a":"<p><code>load_defaults</code> builds a shared config from the default provider chain (environment variables, shared credentials file, SSO or an IAM role) and the configured Region. <code>Client::new(&amp;config)</code> makes the service client from it. <code>BehaviorVersion::latest()</code> opts into current SDK behavior, while pinning a specific version keeps behavior stable across SDK upgrades.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Rust</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>let config = aws_config::load_defaults(aws_config::BehaviorVersion::latest()).await;\nlet client = Client::new(&amp;config);</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"bedrock-f0061","card":"q-bedrock-010-rust-hello-world","sec":"bedrock-s011","q":"In the AWS SDK for Rust <code>invoke_model</code> call, why is the request body wrapped in <code>Blob</code>?","a":"<p><code>InvokeModel</code> takes the body as raw bytes, because the payload format differs per model. <code>serde_json::to_vec</code> turns the JSON value into bytes, and <code>Blob</code> is the SDK's byte type for binary payloads. The <code>?</code> returns early if serialization fails.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Rust</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>.body(Blob::new(serde_json::to_vec(&amp;body)?))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"bedrock-f0062","card":"q-bedrock-010-rust-hello-world","sec":"bedrock-s011","q":"What does the <code>json!</code> macro from <code>serde_json</code> do, and why use it for a request body?","a":"<p><code>json!</code> builds a <code>serde_json::Value</code> from JSON-like syntax inside Rust code, with variable interpolation. It avoids hand-written string escaping and rejects malformed JSON syntax at compile time, while a <code>String</code> template would only fail at run time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0063","card":"q-bedrock-010-rust-hello-world","sec":"bedrock-s011","q":"How does error handling in a Rust Bedrock client differ from a Bash version?","a":"<p>Bash handles errors by checking exit codes manually, and many steps fail silently without <code>set -e</code>. Rust returns <code>Result</code>, and <code>?</code> propagates any error to <code>main</code>, where <code>anyhow::Result</code> reports it and sets a non-zero exit status. Ignoring an error needs an explicit choice.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0064","card":"q-bedrock-010-rust-hello-world","sec":"bedrock-s011","q":"What is the difference between <code>cargo run</code> and <code>cargo build --release</code>, and which do you ship?","a":"<p><code>cargo run</code> builds a debug version (fast to compile, slow to run) and runs it. <code>cargo build --release</code> builds an optimized binary in <code>target/release/</code>, which is the one you distribute and benchmark. The release binary runs without Rust installed, so the target machine needs only AWS credentials.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0065","card":"q-bedrock-010-rust-hello-world","sec":"bedrock-s011","q":"When would you move code from <code>main.rs</code> into <code>lib.rs</code> in a Rust project?","a":"<p>Begin in <code>main.rs</code> while you explore. Once the logic will be reused (a CLI with subcommands, a library, a web service or a Lambda), move it into <code>lib.rs</code> so other binaries and tests can import it, and keep <code>main.rs</code> thin. Doing it too early adds structure before you know the shape.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0066","card":"q-bedrock-010-rust-hello-world","sec":"bedrock-s011","q":"How would you run a Rust Bedrock client in AWS Lambda?","a":"<p>Use Cargo Lambda (<code>cargo lambda build --release</code>, <code>cargo lambda deploy</code>) with the <code>lambda_runtime</code> crate, which compiles to a small binary for the custom runtime. Give the function an execution role that allows <code>bedrock:InvokeModel</code> for the model, and set the Lambda timeout high enough, since model calls can take several seconds.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0067","card":"q-bedrock-011-7-knowledge-bases-and-rag","sec":"bedrock-s012","q":"What is an Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Base, and how does it reduce hallucination?","a":"<p>It is a managed RAG pipeline: it ingests your documents, splits them into chunks, embeds them and stores the vectors, then retrieves relevant chunks at question time and passes them to a model as context. Because the model answers from your data, it hallucinates less and can cite its source. It does not remove hallucination fully, since the model can still misread the context.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0068","card":"q-bedrock-011-7-knowledge-bases-and-rag","sec":"bedrock-s012","q":"What are the steps in the RAG workflow of a Bedrock Knowledge Base, from raw files to an answer?","a":"<ol>\n  <li>Put source documents in a data source such as an S3 bucket. 2) Run a sync (ingestion job) that chunks the files, creates embeddings with an embedding model and writes them to a vector store. 3) At query time, embed the question and retrieve the most similar chunks. 4) Pass the question and chunks to a foundation model, which writes the grounded answer.</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0069","card":"q-bedrock-011-7-knowledge-bases-and-rag","sec":"bedrock-s012","q":"When new documents are added to the S3 bucket behind a knowledge base, why do answers not change until you act, and what must you do?","a":"<p>The vector index is a copy built at ingestion time, so S3 changes are not visible until you start a new sync (ingestion job). Run the sync after each batch of changes, for example from an S3 event or a schedule, otherwise answers go stale.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0070","card":"q-bedrock-011-7-knowledge-bases-and-rag","sec":"bedrock-s012","q":"Which vector stores can back a Bedrock Knowledge Base?","a":"<p>As of 2025, Bedrock can create an Amazon OpenSearch Serverless collection for you, or use your own store such as Aurora PostgreSQL with pgvector, OpenSearch Managed Cluster, Neptune Analytics, Pinecone, Redis Enterprise Cloud, MongoDB Atlas and Amazon S3 Vectors. The list changes, so check the current docs. Choose by existing skills, cost at your scale, latency and filtering needs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0071","card":"q-bedrock-011-7-knowledge-bases-and-rag","sec":"bedrock-s012","q":"How does chunking affect RAG answer quality, and what chunking options does Bedrock Knowledge Bases offer?","a":"<p>Chunks that are too large dilute relevance and waste context, and chunks that are too small lose surrounding meaning. As of 2025, options include default, fixed-size with overlap, hierarchical, semantic, no chunking, and custom chunking with Lambda. Pick by document structure and tune using retrieve results.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0072","card":"q-bedrock-011-7-knowledge-bases-and-rag","sec":"bedrock-s012","q":"A knowledge base returns irrelevant answers or none. What do you check?","a":"<p>Check that the sync finished and the documents were ingested, and that the file formats are supported. Then use retrieve alone to see which chunks come back. If the right passage is not retrieved, change chunk size, add metadata filters, try a better embedding model or hybrid search. If it is retrieved but ignored, fix the prompt or use a stronger model.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0073","card":"q-bedrock-011-7-knowledge-bases-and-rag","sec":"bedrock-s012","q":"Give examples of data that suits a knowledge base and a situation where RAG is the wrong tool.","a":"<p>Good fits are support history, product catalogs, internal documentation, research papers and a proprietary codebase: private, text-heavy data the model has not seen. RAG is a poor choice for questions that need aggregation across the whole dataset (sums, counts, joins), which SQL answers better, or when the data is small enough to paste into the prompt.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0074","card":"q-bedrock-011-7-knowledge-bases-and-rag","sec":"bedrock-s012","q":"How can you restrict which documents a knowledge base query can retrieve for a given user?","a":"<p>Add metadata to documents at ingestion (for example department or access level) and pass a metadata filter on the retrieve call, built from the user's identity. Do the check in application code, because the knowledge base does not know which end user is asking. For strict isolation use separate knowledge bases.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0075","card":"q-bedrock-012-custom-prompt-on-a-knowledge-base","sec":"bedrock-s013","q":"Why would you override the default prompt template of a knowledge base?","a":"<p>The default template is general and long. A custom template lets you set the role, answer format, length, tone and grounding rule (for example \"answer only from the search results, and say you do not know otherwise\"). The trade-off is that the template must still include the placeholders the service fills in, such as <code>$search_results$</code>, or the model gets no context.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0076","card":"q-bedrock-012-custom-prompt-on-a-knowledge-base","sec":"bedrock-s013","q":"Which prompt techniques make a custom knowledge base prompt reliable?","a":"<p>Assign a role, state explicit rules, add a grounding constraint (use only the retrieved results), define the output format, and finish with a few worked question and answer examples. Examples are among the most effective because the model copies their format. Each example costs input tokens.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0077","card":"q-bedrock-012-custom-prompt-on-a-knowledge-base","sec":"bedrock-s013","q":"How do you make a model admit that the answer is not in the retrieved data instead of guessing?","a":"<p>State it as a rule (\"If the search results do not contain the answer, say you do not know\") and give an example of that refusal. Lower the temperature, and consider a Guardrails contextual grounding check that scores the answer against the retrieved text and blocks unsupported answers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0078","card":"q-bedrock-012-custom-prompt-on-a-knowledge-base","sec":"bedrock-s013","q":"You edit the prompt template of a knowledge base and answers look the same. What do you check?","a":"<p>Check that the change was saved and applies to the call you are testing, that the chat was cleared so earlier turns do not influence the answer, that the template keeps the required placeholders, and that you are testing the generation path, not retrieve-only. Also check that no worked example in the prompt conflicts with the rules.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0079","card":"q-bedrock-013-8-programmatic-knowledge-base-queries","sec":"bedrock-s014","q":"Which service and operations do you use to query a knowledge base from code, and why not <code>bedrock-runtime</code>?","a":"<p>Use the <code>bedrock-agent-runtime</code> client with <code>Retrieve</code> and <code>RetrieveAndGenerate</code>. <code>bedrock-runtime</code> only runs models and knows nothing about your knowledge base. The agent runtime does the vector search and, for <code>RetrieveAndGenerate</code>, the model call in one request.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0080","card":"q-bedrock-013-8-programmatic-knowledge-base-queries","sec":"bedrock-s014","q":"How do <code>Retrieve</code> and <code>RetrieveAndGenerate</code> differ, and when do you use each?","a":"<p><code>Retrieve</code> returns the matching chunks, scores and source locations, and you build the prompt and call the model yourself, which gives full control over prompt, model and post-processing. <code>RetrieveAndGenerate</code> does retrieval, prompting and generation in one call and returns text with citations, which is quicker to build. Use <code>Retrieve</code> for custom prompts, reranking or a non-Bedrock model.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0081","card":"q-bedrock-013-8-programmatic-knowledge-base-queries","sec":"bedrock-s014","q":"In boto3, how do you ask a knowledge base a question and read the answer and its citations?","a":"<p>Use <code>retrieve_and_generate</code> on the <code>bedrock-agent-runtime</code> client, as the code shows. <code>input.text</code> holds the question, <code>knowledgeBaseId</code> selects the knowledge base, and <code>modelArn</code> selects the model that writes the answer (a model or inference profile your account can use in that Region). The answer is in <code>output.text</code>, and <code>citations</code> list retrieved passages and their S3 locations.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import boto3\n\nclient = boto3.client(&quot;bedrock-agent-runtime&quot;, region_name=&quot;us-east-1&quot;)\nresponse = client.retrieve_and_generate(\n    input={&quot;text&quot;: &quot;What is Rust?&quot;},\n    retrieveAndGenerateConfiguration={\n        &quot;type&quot;: &quot;KNOWLEDGE_BASE&quot;,\n        &quot;knowledgeBaseConfiguration&quot;: {\n            &quot;knowledgeBaseId&quot;: &quot;KB_ID&quot;,\n            &quot;modelArn&quot;: &quot;MODEL_ARN&quot;,\n        },\n    },\n)\nprint(response[&quot;output&quot;][&quot;text&quot;])\nprint(response[&quot;citations&quot;])</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"bedrock-f0082","card":"q-bedrock-013-8-programmatic-knowledge-base-queries","sec":"bedrock-s014","q":"Which IAM permissions does the caller need to use <code>RetrieveAndGenerate</code> on a knowledge base?","a":"<p>The caller needs <code>bedrock:RetrieveAndGenerate</code>, <code>bedrock:Retrieve</code> on the knowledge base ARN, and <code>bedrock:InvokeModel</code> on the generation model. Separately, the knowledge base's own service role needs access to the S3 data and the embedding model. Scope each to specific ARNs (least privilege).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0083","card":"q-bedrock-013-8-programmatic-knowledge-base-queries","sec":"bedrock-s014","q":"Why is AWS CloudShell a convenient place to prototype Bedrock calls?","a":"<p>CloudShell is a browser shell, already authenticated as your console identity, with the AWS CLI preinstalled, so there are no keys to configure. It suits quick tests. For production, move to a script, SDK or Lambda with a proper IAM role, because CloudShell is interactive and tied to a user.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0084","card":"q-bedrock-013-8-programmatic-knowledge-base-queries","sec":"bedrock-s014","q":"What does piping AWS CLI JSON output into <code>jq</code> give you when testing a knowledge base?","a":"<p><code>jq</code> pretty-prints the JSON and lets you pick fields, for example <code>jq -r '.output.text'</code> for just the answer or <code>.citations</code> to inspect where answers came from. It makes CLI output readable and is useful in scripts that chain commands.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0085","card":"q-bedrock-014-9-agents","sec":"bedrock-s015","q":"What is an Amazon Bedrock Agent and what does it consist of?","a":"<p>An agent is a managed orchestration of a foundation model with instructions, optional action groups (tools backed by Lambda or returned to your app), an optional knowledge base for grounding, and optionally a code interpreter. The model plans steps, calls tools, reads results and responds, looping until the task is done.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0086","card":"q-bedrock-014-9-agents","sec":"bedrock-s015","q":"Why should you keep each agent small and specific?","a":"<p>Narrow agents with clear input and output are easier to prompt, test and debug, and have fewer ways to go wrong, like a small function. For bigger workflows, chain several simple agents instead of building one complex one. The cost is more orchestration and more model calls.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0087","card":"q-bedrock-014-9-agents","sec":"bedrock-s015","q":"How would you design a CI/CD agent loop that fixes a failing build?","a":"<p>Agent 1 reads the build traceback and extracts the error and the relevant code. Agent 2, with the code interpreter, proposes a small fix and corrects the test. Agent 3 re-runs the build. A pass ends the loop and a fail returns to Agent 1. Cap the number of retries and require human review of changes, so it cannot loop forever or push bad code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0088","card":"q-bedrock-014-9-agents","sec":"bedrock-s015","q":"What does the code interpreter option add to an agent, and what is the risk?","a":"<p>It lets the agent write and run code in a sandbox to compute, analyze data or test its own output, so answers rest on executed results, not guesses. The risk is that model-written code can be wrong or unsafe, so keep it sandboxed, limit permissions and check results.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0089","card":"q-bedrock-014-9-agents","sec":"bedrock-s015","q":"What are action groups in a Bedrock Agent?","a":"<p>An action group defines the tools the agent may call, described by an OpenAPI schema or function definitions and backed by a Lambda function (or returned to your application for you to run). The model reads the descriptions to decide when to call an action, so clear descriptions and narrow IAM permissions matter.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0090","card":"q-bedrock-014-9-agents","sec":"bedrock-s015","q":"How do you call a Bedrock Agent from code, and from where can it be invoked?","a":"<p>Use <code>InvokeAgent</code> on the <code>bedrock-agent-runtime</code> client with the agent ID, an alias ID and a session ID. The response is a stream of chunks. Anything that can make an AWS API call can invoke it: scripts, Lambda, containers, or CI/CD pipelines. Reuse the same session ID to keep conversation memory within a session.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0091","card":"q-bedrock-014-9-agents","sec":"bedrock-s015","q":"How can you debug why a Bedrock Agent chose a certain action, and how does it remember a conversation?","a":"<p>Turn on trace when you invoke the agent (<code>enableTrace=True</code> in <code>invoke_agent</code>) to see the model's reasoning steps, the action groups or knowledge bases it called and their results. For memory, calls that reuse the same <code>sessionId</code> share conversation context within the session timeout. Agent memory across sessions is a separate optional feature (as of 2025).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0092","card":"q-bedrock-015-creating-agent-sum","sec":"bedrock-s016","q":"In a Bedrock agent, what do the instructions do, and what should a good set contain?","a":"<p>The instructions are the agent's system prompt: they define its job, rules and output format. A good set states one narrow task, the exact output format (for example \"return only JSON objects\"), and what to do when the input is out of scope (a fixed fallback reply). Specific rules give predictable behavior.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0093","card":"q-bedrock-015-creating-agent-sum","sec":"bedrock-s016","q":"After you edit a Bedrock agent's configuration in the console, why must you choose Prepare before testing?","a":"<p>Saving changes the working draft. Prepare builds the draft version so the test window uses the new instructions, knowledge bases and action groups. If you skip it, you test the old prepared version. To deploy, create a numbered version and an alias that your callers use.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0094","card":"q-bedrock-015-creating-agent-sum","sec":"bedrock-s016","q":"Why tell an agent to return only JSON, and how does that help when other code consumes its output?","a":"<p>Structured output can be parsed and validated by code, so downstream steps do not depend on free text. Combine the instruction with schema validation and a retry on parse failure, because a model can still add text around the JSON.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0095","card":"q-bedrock-015-creating-agent-sum","sec":"bedrock-s016","q":"What do the User Input and Knowledge Bases options on a Bedrock agent each do?","a":"<p>User Input lets the agent ask the user clarifying questions when information is missing. Knowledge Bases lets it retrieve from your vectorized data to ground answers. Enable only what the task needs, since each option adds latency and cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0096","card":"q-bedrock-015-creating-agent-sum","sec":"bedrock-s016","q":"How do you handle an agent given input outside its scope, such as a question with no numbers sent to an agent that only sums numbers?","a":"<p>Put a fixed fallback reply in the instructions (\"If no numbers are present, respond with a short statement of what you do\"). That keeps behavior predictable instead of letting the model improvise, and your calling code can detect the fallback.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0097","card":"q-bedrock-016-10-agents-with-knowledge-bases","sec":"bedrock-s017","q":"What does attaching a knowledge base to a Bedrock agent change about its answers?","a":"<p>Without it, the agent answers from the model's training data with no citations. With a knowledge base, the agent retrieves from your data, grounds the answer in it and returns citations that link to the source files in S3. That lowers hallucination and lets users verify answers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0098","card":"q-bedrock-016-10-agents-with-knowledge-bases","sec":"bedrock-s017","q":"Why are citations important in a RAG application?","a":"<p>Citations make answers traceable: a reader can open the source and check the claim, which builds trust and helps debugging and audits. Without them you cannot tell grounded answers from model guesses.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0099","card":"q-bedrock-016-10-agents-with-knowledge-bases","sec":"bedrock-s017","q":"When should you use an agent with a knowledge base instead of a plain knowledge base query?","a":"<p>Use a plain <code>RetrieveAndGenerate</code> when the task is one question answered from your data. Use an agent when the task needs several steps, tool calls, clarifying questions or a decision about when to retrieve. The agent adds flexibility but also more latency, cost and less predictable behavior.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0100","card":"q-bedrock-016-10-agents-with-knowledge-bases","sec":"bedrock-s017","q":"How do you attach a knowledge base to an existing Bedrock agent, and what role do the knowledge base instructions play?","a":"<p>In the agent builder, add the knowledge base and write instructions that tell the agent when and how to use it, then save. The agent decides from those instructions whether to query the knowledge base for a given question, so vague instructions lead to missed or needless retrievals.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"bedrock-f0101","card":"q-bedrock-017-11-course-wrap-up","sec":"bedrock-s018","q":"What best practices apply when moving a Bedrock prototype to production?","a":"<p>Prototype with the CLI and console, then move to a compiled or SDK-based client. Add error handling, retries, logging and monitoring. Version, lint and test prompts like code, and validate output with JSON or XML checks. Use Compare Mode for ad hoc quality checks.</p>","hand":true}];
